# Tenancy seams (ADR-010)

This app is single-tenant today and must stay cheap to make multi-tenant later. Do not build SaaS features; do honor these seams in all new and touched code. Full rationale: [docs/architecture/adr/010-saas-evolution-strategy.md](../../docs/architecture/adr/010-saas-evolution-strategy.md).

## 1. Prisma access only in `src/lib` domain modules

No new `@/lib/db` imports in `src/app` pages, server actions, or route handlers (ESLint enforces this; nine legacy files are grandfathered in `eslint.config.mjs`). When touching a grandfathered file for feature work, move its queries into the relevant `src/lib` module and remove it from the grandfather list.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — in src/app/blocks/[blockId]/page.tsx
const block = await db.block.findUnique({ where: { blockId } });

// ✅ GOOD — page calls src/lib/blocks/queries.ts
const block = await getBlockDetail(blockId);
```

## 2. Every domain mutation takes `DomainContext`

Per ADR-002 (`src/lib/context/domain-context.ts`). `tenantId` will later be added to this one interface, so nothing may bypass it.

## 3. One credentials seam

No new direct `process.env` reads for channel/marketplace credentials at call sites. Route new channel work through a single `getChannelConfig(ctx, channel)` accessor (replaces `getManaPoolConfigFromEnv()` when channel code is next touched; CHN-001 at the latest). Credentials move from env to per-tenant storage behind this seam later.

## 4. No new global singletons or durable local state

ID sequences and settings stay as DB rows (they become per-tenant rows later). No in-process counters, no filesystem writes the app depends on, no module-level mutable caches of business data.

## 5. Destructive operations take an explicit scope argument

Anything that bulk-deletes or restores (`src/lib/data-reset.ts`, backup restore) must state its scope in its signature, even while the only scope is the whole database. "Wipe everything" must never be implicit.

## 6. Inbound mutation surfaces fail closed

Webhook and cron routes must refuse requests when their verification secret is unconfigured (SAS-001). Never add an inbound route that mutates data without signature or secret verification.
